This chapter is for a mathematician who has not seen the library. It recalls what resolution of
singularities and principalization are, lists the resolution theorems that the library states, and
explains how to read the rest of the document.

# Resolution of singularities
%%%
tag := "intro-resolution"
%%%

Let $`X` be an algebraic variety over a field $`k` of characteristic zero. Most points of $`X` are
smooth, but some may be singular, like the vertex of the cone $`x^2 + y^2 = z^2` or the origin of
the cusp $`y^2 = x^3`. A *resolution of singularities* of $`X` is a proper morphism
$`\pi \colon X' \to X` from a smooth variety $`X'` that is an isomorphism over a dense open subset
of $`X`: the singular variety is replaced by a smooth one that differs from it only over a nowhere
dense set. In the *strong* form {cite Kol07}[(3)], $`\pi` is an isomorphism over the whole smooth locus
$`X^{ns}`, and the preimage $`\pi^{-1}(\operatorname{Sing} X)` of the singular locus is a divisor
with simple normal crossings: near every point of $`X'` there are local coordinates
$`z_1, \dots, z_n` in which it is a union of coordinate hyperplanes $`z_i = 0`
({ref "sec-normal-crossings"}[Normal crossings]).

Hironaka proved in 1964 that every variety over a field of characteristic zero has a resolution,
together with a number of stronger statements {cite Hir64}[]. His resolution, and every one since, is
built from *blow-ups*. The blow-up of a smooth variety along a smooth closed subvariety $`Z`
replaces every point of $`Z` by the projective space of the directions normal to $`Z` at that point;
it changes nothing away from $`Z`, and the preimage of $`Z` becomes a smooth hypersurface, the
*exceptional divisor*. For the cusp, one blow-up of the plane at the origin turns the curve into a
smooth one ({ref "sec-cusp"}[Example: resolving the cusp]). In general one needs a finite succession
of blow-ups $`X_r \to X_{r-1} \to \cdots \to X_0`, each centre chosen in the previous stage
({ref "sec-successions"}[Finite successions of blow-ups]), and the difficulty of the theorem is to
choose the centres so that the process ends with something smooth.

Two variants recur. In *embedded* resolution the singular variety $`Y` lies in a smooth ambient
variety $`X`, and one blows up $`X` along smooth centres until the strict transform of $`Y` (the
closure of the part of its preimage lying off the exceptional divisors) is smooth and crosses the
exceptional divisors normally. In the *analytic* setting the same questions are asked of real- and
complex-analytic manifolds and spaces; a space that is not compact may need infinitely many
blow-ups, finitely many over each compact subset.

# Principalization
%%%
tag := "intro-principalization"
%%%

The proofs reduce resolution to a problem about ideals. Let $`I` be a sheaf of ideals on a smooth
variety $`X`, locally generated by functions $`f_1, \dots, f_m`. A *principalization* of $`I` is a
finite succession of blow-ups with smooth centres, with composite $`\Pi \colon X_r \to X`, such that
the pulled-back ideal $`\Pi^* I` is the ideal sheaf of a divisor with simple normal crossings:
near every point of $`X_r` it is generated by a single monomial $`z_1^{a_1} \cdots z_n^{a_n}` in
suitable local coordinates. Principalizing the ideal of a subvariety $`Y` of a smooth $`X`, and
stopping at the right moment, gives an embedded resolution of $`Y`; and since every variety is
locally a subvariety of a smooth one, a resolution of any variety can be glued from such local
pieces, provided the choices are compatible {cite Kol07}[§3].

Principalization proceeds by *order reduction*. The order of $`I` at a point $`x` is the largest
$`d` with $`I_x \subseteq \mathfrak{m}_x^d`, the vanishing order of the generic member of $`I` at
$`x`. If $`I` has order $`d` along a smooth centre and one blows it up, the pulled-back ideal
factors as the $`d`-th power of the ideal of the exceptional divisor times an ideal called the
*weak transform* of $`I`. With well-chosen centres the maximal order of the weak transform drops
after finitely many blow-ups. Hironaka's Main Theorem II is one round of this, which lowers the
maximal order; his Main Theorem II(N) repeats it until the weak transform is the unit ideal, so
that $`\Pi^* I` is a product of powers of the exceptional divisors. Throughout, the exceptional
divisors accumulated so far, together with a *boundary* divisor given at the start, are kept in
simple normal crossings with each other and with the next centre.

# Functorial resolution
%%%
tag := "intro-functorial"
%%%

Hironaka's theorems assert that a resolution exists. Later proofs make every choice by an
algorithm and give a stronger statement: one rule assigns a succession of blow-ups to every
variety at once, compatibly with smooth morphisms and with change of the base field. Kollár's
functorial principalization and resolution {cite Kol07}[Theorems 35 and 36], Włodarczyk's embedded
desingularization {cite Wlo05}[Theorem 1.0.2] and his principalization on analytic manifolds
{cite Wlo09}[Theorem 2.0.3] are stated in this form ({ref "sec-functors"}[Blow-up sequence
functors]). What the compatibility says is explained with functorial principalization
({bpref "def:commutes_smooth"}[]), and why it amounts to Kollár's resolution functor with
functorial resolution ({ref "sec-resolution"}[Functorial resolution]).

# The resolution theorems of the library
%%%
tag := "intro-theorems"
%%%

The library states the following resolution theorems, each with its source. Each is stated in the
form its source gives it, as far as Lean and Mathlib allow; where the formal statement differs from
the printed one, the difference and the reason for it are recorded at the theorem
({ref "intro-reading"}[How to read a node]).

In Mathlib's language ({ref "ch-standard"}[Resolution of singularities in Mathlib's language]):

- Hironaka's Main Theorem I: resolution of an integral scheme of finite type over a field $`k` of
  characteristic zero by a single blow-up along an ideal sheaf supported on the singular locus,
  from a scheme smooth over $`k`, with blow-ups given by their universal property
  ({bpref "thm:standard_algebraic"}[]);
- the local monomialization of a real-analytic function on an open subset of $`\mathbb{R}^n`,
  with the Jacobian determinant in the same coordinates {cite Ati70}[]
  ({bpref "thm:standard_analytic"}[]).

Over a field $`k` of characteristic zero, for schemes of finite type
({ref "ch-algebraic"}[Algebraic schemes]):

- Hironaka's Main Theorem II, one round of order reduction ({bpref "thm:mt2"}[]), and Main
  Theorem II(N), principalization of an ideal sheaf on a smooth scheme ({bpref "thm:mt2n"}[]);
- Hironaka's Corollary 1, which makes one of finitely many ideal sheaves the unit ideal at every
  point ({bpref "thm:cor1"}[]), and Corollary 3, which turns the inverse image of a nowhere dense
  closed subscheme into a normal crossings divisor ({bpref "thm:cor3"}[]);
- Kollár's functorial principalization ({bpref "thm:principalization"}[]) and functorial
  resolution ({bpref "thm:resolution"}[]), and his strong resolution of a variety
  ({bpref "thm:strong_resolution"}[]);
- Hironaka's Main Theorem I, which resolves a reduced and irreducible scheme by one blow-up with
  centre supported on the singular locus ({bpref "thm:mt1"}[]), and its weak form
  ({bpref "thm:mt1_weak"}[]);
- Włodarczyk's embedded desingularization with smooth centres, in functorial form
  ({bpref "thm:embedded"}[]).

For real- and complex-analytic manifolds and spaces
({ref "ch-analytic"}[Analytic manifolds and analytic spaces]):

- Hironaka's principalization on a real-analytic manifold, Main Theorem II″(N)
  ({bpref "thm:mt2pp"}[]), and Bierstone–Milman's principalization with a condition on the
  Jacobian ideal ({bpref "thm:bm"}[]), both stated over every compact subset, compatibly;
- Włodarczyk's principalization on a real- or complex-analytic manifold, in functorial form: one
  rule for all manifolds of a given dimension, compatible with local analytic isomorphisms
  ({bpref "thm:wlo09_principalization"}[]);
- Włodarczyk's embedded desingularization of a closed analytic subspace of a manifold
  ({bpref "thm:wlo09_embedded"}[]);
- the resolution of reduced real- and complex-analytic spaces after Kollár and Włodarczyk
  ({bpref "thm:spaces"}[]), and its form for a single reduced complex space
  ({bpref "thm:spaces_complex"}[]).

Every theorem is proved in the library and checked by Lean's kernel, with no axioms beyond the
three standard ones of Lean and Mathlib; each is followed by a short sketch of the route its proof
takes.

# How to read a node
%%%
tag := "intro-reading"
%%%

Each main theorem, and each definition its statement uses, is a numbered node. The statement at
the top is written by hand, in the source's notation; the Lean declaration below it is what Lean
checks.

The boxes under a node record how the Lean statement relates to its source. A *Translation*
matches Lean notation with the source's. *Unformalised* marks a source item with no Lean counterpart
yet, *Out of scope* one left out by decision. A *Correction* repairs a claim false as printed; an
*Interpretation* fixes one reading of underspecified wording. A *Restatement* is an equivalent form,
a *Strengthening* implies the source's statement, and a *Gap* is weaker or incomparable; a node with
a gap shows "Owes work". A *Formalisation note* is a remark that fits none of these.

Conventions shared by the statements, such as how an algebraic $`k`-scheme is given to Lean, are in
the appendix ({ref "ch-conventions"}[Conventions and sources]).

# Where to start
%%%
tag := "intro-start"
%%%

- *New to schemes or blow-ups*: the dictionary in the appendix gives the Lean name and the node of
  each classical notion ({ref "conv-dictionary"}[Dictionary]), and the chapter on the blow-up
  states its universal property and resolves the cusp
  ({ref "ch-scheme-blow-up"}[The blow-up along an ideal sheaf]); then Main Theorems II and
  II(N) ({bpref "thm:mt2"}[], {bpref "thm:mt2n"}[]), whose conditions are the classical ones.
- *Resolution of a variety in its usual form*: Kollár's strong resolution
  ({bpref "thm:strong_resolution"}[]), then his functorial resolution ({bpref "thm:resolution"}[])
  and what its compatibility with smooth morphisms means ({bpref "def:commutes_smooth"}[],
  {ref "sec-resolution"}[Functorial resolution]).
- *Hironaka's paper*: Main Theorems II and II(N), Corollaries 1 and 3, and Main Theorem I with its
  weak form, all in the chapter on the algebraic theorems; Main Theorem II″(N) opens the chapter on
  the analytic theorems ({bpref "thm:mt2pp"}[]).
- *The analytic theorems*: the opening of their chapter explains the compatible families over the
  compact subsets of a manifold in which the manifold statements are made
  ({ref "ch-analytic"}[Analytic manifolds and analytic spaces]).
- *A particular definition*: the chapters on schemes and on analytic geometry are grouped by
  topic, the properties that a theorem asserts are stated in its section, and the chip "uses" of
  a theorem lists the definitions its statement depends on. The dependency graph and the summary
  at the end of the document are computed from the Lean declarations.
